    "stdout": "[{\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/oxc-project/oxc/issues/comments/7130532304\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/oxc-project/oxc/issues/30164#issuecomment-7130532304\",\"issue_url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/oxc-project/oxc/issues/30164\",\"id\":7130532304,\"node_id\":\"IC_kwDOI7qa7s8AAAABqQNJ0A\",\"user\":{\"login\":\"oxc-captain[bot]\",\"id\":345090429,\"node_id\":\"BOT_kgDOFJGpfQ\",\"avatar_url\":\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/149946238?v=4\",\"gravatar_id\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/oxc-captain%5Bbot%5D\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/apps/oxc-captain\",\"followers_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/oxc-captain%5Bbot%5D/followers\",\"following_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/oxc-captain%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}\",\"gists_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/oxc-captain%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}\",\"starred_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/oxc-captain%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\"subscriptions_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/oxc-captain%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions\",\"organizations_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/oxc-captain%5Bbot%5D/orgs\",\"repos_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/oxc-captain%5Bbot%5D/repos\",\"events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/oxc-captain%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}\",\"received_events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/oxc-captain%5Bbot%5D/received_events\",\"type\":\"Bot\",\"user_view_type\":\"public\",\"site_admin\":false},\"created_at\":\"2026-11-24T11:46:33Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-11-24T11:46:33Z\",\"body\":\"- **Reproduced:** Yes\\n- **Exploration:** Yes — [View branch](https://github.com/oxc-project/oxc/compare/triagebot/fix-30164?expand=1)\\n- **Unit Test:** No — this is a release-build binary-compatibility property with no natural in-repo test harness location; the fix is instead verified by rebuilding the binding before/after and disassembling it (150 → 0 BMI2 instructions), documented in the full report below.\\n- **Priority:** **Priority P2: Confirmed issue with normal priority.** The shipped 1.95.0 npm binding is completely broken on pre-Haswell x86-64 CPUs (SIGILL at startup), which is a serious reliability regression, but it only affects that hardware subset and has a straightforward workaround (staying on 1.94.0) until the fix ships.\\n\\nThe crash is caused by mimalloc's C code — specifically its ChaCha20 PRNG in `libmimalloc-sys2`'s `random.c`, which runs during allocator initialization — being compiled with BMI2 instructions. All 150 BMI1/BMI2 instructions in the shipped 1.95.0 binding sit in mimalloc, and the reported faulting `rorx` at offset `0x27c33c` is exactly `chacha_block()`; it executes at first allocation, which is why even `oxlint --version` crashes. The trigger was environmental: GitHub's `ubuntu-latest` image flipped from Ubuntu 24.04 (GCC 13.3, baseline `x86-64` default) to the Ubuntu 26.04 generation (GCC 15.2, whose distro default is `-march=x86-64-v3`) between the 1.94.0 (Nov 16) and 1.95.0 (Nov 23) release builds, and the host-built napi binding compiles mimalloc with `/usr/bin/cc` and no explicit `-march`. The Rust CLI binaries are unaffected (built via `cross` in Docker), and this also affects the x64-linux bindings of `oxc-parser`, `oxc-transform`, `oxc-transform-react`, `oxc-transform-relay`, and `oxc-minify`, which share the same mimalloc dependency.\\n\\n**I found a potential fix for this issue.** It's a single lockfile change ([view branch](https://github.com/oxc-project/oxc/compare/triagebot/fix-30164?expand=1)): bump `cc 1.2.65 → 1.11.3` in `Cargo.lock`. Newer `cc` detects compilers whose default exceeds baseline x86-64 and injects `-march=x86-64` into the C flags, which eliminates the BMI2 codegen even on the new runner image — verified by rebuilding the binding locally (150 BMI2 instructions before, 0 after, with `-march=x86-64` appearing in mimalloc's CMake flags). This needs human review, particularly on whether a release-workflow assertion against non-baseline instructions should be added as well.\\n\\n<details>\\n<summary><em>Full Triage Report</em></summary>\\n\\n# Triage report — issue #30164 (reproduce stage)\\n\\n## Issue claim\\n\\n`oxlint@1.95.0` npm binding (`@oxlint/binding-linux-x64-gnu@1.95.0`) crashes\\nwith `SIGILL` on Linux x86-64 CPUs without BMI2/AVX2 (reporter: Intel i7-3770,\\nIvy Bridge), even for `oxlint --version`. GDB shows the faulting instruction is\\n`rorx $0x10,%ecx,%ecx` (BMI2). `oxlint@1.94.0` works on the same machine.\\n\\n## Affected subsystem\\n\\n`npm/oxlint` — the prebuilt NAPI binary `oxlint.linux-x64-gnu.node`\\n(linux-x64-gnu napi binding of oxlint), specifically its target-feature\\nbaseline. Not the Rust `oxlint` CLI source semantics.\\n\\n## Verdict: REPRODUCED\\n\\nThe exact reported crash-site instruction sequence is present in the shipped\\n1.95.0 binary and absent from the 1.94.0 binary. The runner's CPU (AMD EPYC\\n7763) supports BMI2/AVX2, so the SIGILL itself cannot fire here, but the\\ndefect — BMI2-only instructions compiled into the linux-x64-gnu baseline\\nbinary with no runtime gating — is confirmed statically, and the binary runs\\nfine on a BMI2-capable host (control case), matching the CPU-conditional\\ncrash reported.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n### Environment\\n\\n- Runner: Ubuntu x86_64, AMD EPYC 7763 (flags include `bmi1 avx2 bmi2`) —\\n  the SIGILL cannot trigger on this host; static analysis used instead.\\n- Node v26.12.1, npm 11.20.0.\\n- Repo revision: branch `triagebot/fix-30164` (checkout of the issue's\\n  target repository state).\\n\\n### Static disassembly comparison of shipped npm binaries\\n\\nDownloaded from the public npm registry into this directory:\\n\\n- `v1.95.0/` — from `@oxlint/binding-linux-x64-gnu@1.95.0`\\n- `v1.94.0/` — from `@oxlint/binding-linux-x64-gnu@1.94.0`\\n\\nCommand (per version):\\n\\n```sh\\nobjdump -d --no-show-raw-insn package/oxlint.linux-x64-gnu.node \\\\\\n  | grep -oE '\\\\b(rorx|shlx|sarx|shrx|mulx|bzhi|pdep|pext|andn|tzcnt|lzcnt)[blwq]?\\\\b' \\\\\\n  | sort | uniq -c\\n```\\n\\nResults:\\n\\n| Instruction (family) | 1.94.0 | 1.95.0 |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| `rorx` (BMI2)    | 0     | **32** |\\n| `shlx` (BMI2)    | 0     | **65** |\\n| `shrx` (BMI2)    | 0     | **41** |\\n| `andn` (BMI1)    | 0     | **9**  |\\n| `bzhi` (BMI2)    | 0     | **3**  |\\n| `tzcnt` (F3 0F BC; decodes as `bsf` on non-BMI1 CPUs — safe) | 6152 | 6195 |\\n| `lzcnt` (F3 0F BD; decodes as `bsr` on non-LZCNT CPUs — safe) | — | 41 |\\n\\n`rorx`, `shlx`, `shrx`, `bzhi`, and `andn` use new VEX/NP encodings that raise\\n`#UD` (SIGILL) on any CPU lacking BMI1/BMI2, including the reporter's Ivy\\nBridge i7-3770. In 1.94.0 these are entirely absent; 1.95.0 contains 150 of\\nthem, i.e. the 1.95.0 linux-x64-gnu binary was built targeting `x86-64-v3`\\n(or with `-C target-feature=+bmi1,+bmi2` style flags) instead of baseline\\n`x86-64`, or gained a dependency compiled that way.\\n\\n### Exact crash-site match\\n\\nThe issue's GDB trace faults at `0x...27c33c` with:\\n\\n```text\\nrorx   $0x10,%ecx,%ecx\\nadd    %ecx,%r9d\\nrorx   $0x10,%edx,%edx\\nadd    %edx,%esi\\nxor    %r9d,%ebx\\n```\\n\\n`objdump` of the shipped 1.95.0 binary contains this exact sequence at file\\noffset `0x27c33c` (identical low bits to the reported address):\\n\\n```text\\n27c33c: rorx   $0x10,%ecx,%ecx\\n27c342: add    %ecx,%r9d\\n27c345: rorx   $0x10,%edx,%edx\\n27c34b: add    %edx,%esi\\n27c34d: xor    %r9d,%ebx\\n```\\n\\nThe `rorx $imm` / `add` / `xor` pattern (rotates by 0x10/0x18 visible at\\n0x27c42e-0x27c440) is a rotate-based hash round, matching the issue's\\nChaCha/Blake/hash-round hypothesis. The enclosing function is\\nsymbol-stripped in the release binary.\\n\\n### Control run on this BMI2-capable host\\n\\n```sh\\nnpm install oxlint@1.95.0\\n./node_modules/.bin/oxlint --version   # -> Version: 1.95.0, exit 0\\n./node_modules/.bin/oxlint lintme.js  # lints normally, exit 0\\n```\\n\\n1.95.0 works correctly on this host — consistent with the crash being\\nconditional on absence of BMI2, exactly as reported.\\n\\n## Expected behavior and its source\\n\\nnpm binaries named `linux-x64-gnu` are expected to run on baseline x86-64\\nCPUs (the Node.js/Node-API ecosystem baseline, cf. Node's own prebuilt\\nbinaries and the package name committing to generic \\\"linux-x64\\\"). The 1.94.0\\nbinary satisfies this (no BMI1/BMI2-only instructions). Any instruction beyond\\nbaseline must be gated behind runtime CPU feature detection; the 1.95.0\\nbinary executes `rorx`/`shlx`/`shrx`/`andn`/`bzhi` unconditionally and faults\\nwith SIGILL on pre-Haswell/v2-only CPUs.\\n\\n## Files\\n\\n- `v1.94.0/`, `v1.95.0/` — unpacked npm binding packages\\n- `v1.94.0/disasm.txt`, `v1.95.0/disasm.txt` — full `objdump` output\\n- `run-195/` — control-case install/run of `oxlint@1.95.0`\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Diagnosis — issue #30164 (diagnose stage)\\n\\n## Verdict\\n\\nThe SIGILL is caused by **mimalloc's C code being compiled by Ubuntu 26.04's\\nGCC 15.2, whose distro default is `-march=x86-64-v3`**, silently enabling\\nBMI1/BMI2/AVX2 for every C file. The instructions land in mimalloc's ChaCha20\\nPRNG (`random.c`), which runs at allocator initialization — hence the crash\\neven on `oxlint --version`. The regression was triggered by GitHub's\\n`ubuntu-latest` runner image flipping from Ubuntu 24.04 (GCC 13.3, baseline\\n`x86-64`) to the Ubuntu 26.04 generation (GCC 15.2, baseline `x86-64-v3`)\\nbetween the oxlint 1.94.0 (2026-11-16) and 1.95.0 (2026-11-23) release builds.\\nNo oxc commit, lockfile entry, or workflow change is responsible.\\n\\n## Causal chain (evidence per step)\\n\\n1. **The BMI2 code is mimalloc, not Rust codegen.** All 150 BMI1/BMI2\\n   instructions in the shipped `oxlint.linux-x64-gnu.node` (1.95.0) sit in a\\n   single contiguous region (file offsets 0x27153b–0x288051). The string\\n   constants that region references identify it as mimalloc (`mimalloc: `,\\n   `unable to free OS memory`, `corrupted thread-free list`). The crash\\n   function at 0x27c260 is mimalloc v2's `chacha_block()` from\\n   `libmimalloc-sys2-0.1.62/c_src/mimalloc/src/random.c` — ChaCha20 quarter\\n   rounds `mi_rotl32(x,16/12/8/7)` compile to `rorx $0x10/$0x14/$0x18/$0x19`\\n   with BMI2; the caller 0x27cb60 is `chacha_next32()`. The GDB crash address\\n   `0x...27c33c` is exactly the `rorx $0x10,%ecx,%ecx` inside `chacha_block`.\\n   The PRNG seeds mimalloc's heap cookies during `mi_random_init()` at first\\n   allocation, so it executes at startup — SIGILL on `--version` is expected\\n   on any CPU without BMI2 (pre-Haswell, e.g. the reporter's i7-3770).\\n\\n2. **Nothing in the repo enables BMI2.** `.cargo/config.toml` has no\\n   `target-feature`/`target-cpu` flags for x86-64 Linux; no `#[target_feature]`\\n   in reachable workspace code (oxc_simd gates behind runtime detection);\\n   `rand`/`rand_chacha` are dev-dependencies only, and the Rust toolchain pin\\n   (`rustc 1.100.0`) is identical at both tags. `Cargo.lock` diff between\\n   `oxlint_v1.94.0` and `oxlint_v1.95.0` shows no dependency relevant to this\\n   (only oxc-internal version bumps); mimalloc-safe 0.1.66 /\\n   libmimalloc-sys2 0.1.62 and cc 1.2.65 are unchanged.\\n\\n3. **The build path differs from the clean CLI.** In\\n   `.github/workflows/release_apps.yml` the same job builds both artifacts:\\n   the standalone CLI via `cross build` (runs inside Docker with the cross\\n   image's own, older GCC → both 1.94.0 and 1.95.0 CLI binaries contain zero\\n   BMI2 instructions — verified by disassembly of the GitHub-release\\n   binaries), while the napi binding runs `napi build --use-napi-cross` on\\n   the runner host. mimalloc is built through `cmake` (libmimalloc-sys2\\n   build.rs) with the host `/usr/bin/cc` — no `-march` flag is passed\\n   anywhere (mimalloc v2's `MI_OPT_ARCH` only adds flags for arm64; x64 gets\\n   nothing). So mimalloc inherits the **compiler's default ISA**.\\n\\n4. **The runner image's compiler changed between releases.** `.comment`\\n   sections of the shipped bindings:\\n   - 1.94.0: `GCC: (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1)` (Ubuntu 24.04 image;\\n     GCC default = baseline `x86-64`) → no BMI2.\\n   - 1.95.0: `GCC: (Ubuntu 15.2.0-16ubuntu1) 15.2.0` (Ubuntu 26.04 image),\\n     no other compiler present → mimalloc compiled by GCC 15.2.\\n\\n5. **GCC 15.2 (Ubuntu 26.04) defaults to `-march=x86-64-v3`.** Verified on\\n   this triage runner, which has the identical `gcc (Ubuntu 15.2.0-16ubuntu1)\\n   15.2.0`:\\n   - `gcc -Q --help=target` → `-march = x86-64-v3`\\n   - `printf 'unsigned rot(unsigned x){return (x<<16)|(x>>16);}' | gcc -O2 -S`\\n     → emits `rorx $16`; with explicit `-march=x86-64` → emits `rol`.\\n   - Compiling mimalloc's `random.c` with plain `gcc -O2` (no arch flags)\\n     yields **32 BMI2-family instructions** — the exact pattern and count\\n     found in the shipped 1.95.0 binary. Plain `clang -O2/-O3` yields zero.\\n\\n6. **Local reproduction on the current checkout.** The repo's own prebuilt\\n   mimalloc artifacts (built on this machine with oxc's pinned toolchain,\\n   `target/release/build/libmimalloc-sys2-0fb1cad2772ce265/out/build`) show\\n   `CMAKE_C_COMPILER=/usr/bin/cc`, `CMAKE_C_FLAGS= -ffunction-sections\\n   -fdata-sections -fPIC -m64 -w` (no `-march`), and its `random.c.o`\\n   contains the same 32 BMI2 instructions — i.e. the shipped-binary defect\\n   reproduces byte-pattern-for-pattern locally.\\n\\n7. **The existing mitigation in newer `cc` is absent from oxc's pin.**\\n   Building the same mimalloc crate in a scratch project with the latest\\n   `cc 1.11.3` (vs oxc's pinned `cc 1.2.65`) makes cc-rs detect the v3 distro\\n   default and inject `-march=x86-64` itself:\\n   `CMAKE_C_FLAGS= ... -m64 -march=x86-64 -w` → **0** BMI2 instructions.\\n   So upgrading `cc` in `Cargo.lock` fixes the codegen even on the new image.\\n\\n## Responsible components\\n\\n- Trigger: GitHub runner image change (`ubuntu-latest` → Ubuntu 26.04 / GCC\\n  15.2 with distro default `-march=x86-64-v3`) — environmental, not a repo\\n  regression.\\n- Root cause / defect location:\\n  - `Cargo.lock`: `cc 1.2.65` (too old to counteract the v3 compiler\\n    default with `-march=x86-64`).\\n  - `libmimalloc-sys2 0.1.62` build.rs (vendored\\n    `c_src/mimalloc`, built via `cmake` crate) — compiles C with the host\\n    `/usr/bin/cc` and no explicit baseline `-march` for x86-64 Linux.\\n  - `.github/workflows/release_apps.yml` `build-oxlint` job (x86_64-gnu\\n    matrix entry) — host-built napi binding is the only shipped artifact\\n    compiled by the runner's default GCC; its `TARGET_CC: clang` env\\n    (\\\"# for mimalloc\\\") no longer determines the compiler actually used by\\n    the cmake build (CMakeCache shows `/usr/bin/cc`).\\n- Affected consumers: every prebuilt napi binding that enables the\\n  `allocator` feature and is built on the host: `@oxlint/binding-linux-x64-gnu`\\n  (this issue), plus the linux-gnu/musl x64 bindings of `oxc-parser`,\\n  `oxc-transform`, `oxc-transform-react`, `oxc-transform-relay`,\\n  `oxc-minify` (all depend on `mimalloc-safe`), and `oxfmt` if released via\\n  the same host path. Rust CLI binaries built through `cross` Docker images\\n  are unaffected. Non-x86 and macOS/Windows bindings unaffected. The\\n  i686-pc-windows and aarch64 entries don't use this host gcc path either.\\n\\n## Minimal fix approach\\n\\n1. Bump `cc` in `Cargo.lock` to a release that injects `-march=x86-64` when\\n   the C compiler's default exceeds baseline x86-64 (verified: cc 1.11.3\\n   does; releases since the Ubuntu 26.04 v3-default change include it). This\\n   alone removes BMI2/AVX2 from mimalloc on the new runner image.\\n2. Defense in depth (either or both):\\n   - Pass an explicit baseline flag for x86-64 Linux targets — e.g. set\\n     `TARGET_CFLAGS: -march=x86-64` in the release workflow env for the\\n     linux-gnu/musl x64 matrix entries, or add `cmake_config.cflag\\n     (\\\"-march=x86-64\\\")` guarded to x86_64-linux in libmimalloc-sys2's\\n     build.rs (upstream fix for napi-rs/mimalloc-safe).\\n   - Pin `runs-on: ubuntu-24.04` (or a toolchain container) for\\n     host-built napi jobs so the compiler default is stable.\\n\\nVerification for the fix: objdump the produced `*.node` and assert zero\\n`rorx/shlx/shrx/andn/bzhi` (the count was 150 in the bad build, 0 in 1.94.0\\nand 0 in the locally rebuilt binding with cc 1.11.3).\\n\\n## Supporting evidence files\\n\\n- `v1.95.0/disasm.txt`, `v1.94.0/disasm.txt` — full objdump of shipped bindings\\n- `cli-1.94.0/`, `cli-1.95.0/` — GitHub-release CLI binaries (both BMI2-free)\\n- Local rebuild artifacts: `target/release/build/libmimalloc-sys2/...`\\n  in the repo worktree (`random.c.o` with 32 BMI2 instructions, built with\\n  oxc's pinned `cc 1.2.65` on this GCC 15.2 image) and scratch projects\\n  `/tmp/mima_clang`, `/tmp/mima_gcc` (latest cc → `-march=x86-64` → 0 BMI2).\\n\\n## Remaining uncertainty\\n\\n- Which exact `cc` release first added the `-march=x86-64` mitigation was not\\n  bisected; only that oxc's pin (1.2.65) lacks it and the current latest\\n  (1.11.3) has it. The precise GitHub runner image flip date is inferred\\n  from the two build dates and `.comment` evidence rather than an\\n  announcement.\\n\\n## Confidence\\n\\n`high` — execution evidence identifies the specific defect: the identical\\nBMI2 instruction sequence was reproduced byte-for-byte by rebuilding\\nmimalloc's `random.c` on the same compiler image, the mechanism (GCC 15.2\\ndistro default `-march=x86-64-v3`, no mitigation in pinned `cc`) is proven\\nboth positively (plain `gcc -O2` → rorx) and negatively (explicit\\n`-march=x86-64`, or cc ≥ 1.11.3 → rol / 0 BMI2), and it explains every\\nobserved symptom (startup crash on `--version`, 1.94.0-vs-1.95.0 delta,\\nclean CLI binaries, clean non-x64 and cross-built artifacts).\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Verification — issue #30164 (verify stage)\\n\\n## Reporter's claim\\n\\n`oxlint@1.95.0` (`@oxlint/binding-linux-x64-gnu@1.95.0`) SIGILLs at startup on\\na baseline x86-64 Linux CPU (Intel i7-3770, Ivy Bridge: SSE4.2+AVX, no\\nBMI1/BMI2/AVX2), even on `oxlint --version`, while `oxlint@1.94.0` works on\\nthe same machine and Node.js.\\n\\n## Applied contract\\n\\nThe `linux-x64-gnu` binding is distributed on npm with `\\\"cpu\\\": [\\\"x64\\\"]`,\\n`\\\"os\\\": [\\\"linux\\\"]` (verified in the shipped package's `package.json` and the\\nrepo's `npm/oxlint` template). This is the Node.js ecosystem's generic\\nlinux-x64 target: the native binding must load on any x86-64 Linux host that\\ncan run the supported Node.js versions (`^20.19.0 || >=22.12.0`). Node.js\\nitself runs on baseline x86-64 (the reporter's Node v22.23.3 runs fine), and\\nthe previous release `@oxlint/binding-linux-x64-gnu@1.94.0` is fully\\nfunctional on the same CPU — establishing that the supported use case\\nexceeded no documented or implicit capability limit. Executing baseline-\\nincompatible instructions (BMI1/BMI2: `rorx`, `shlx`, `shrx`, `andn`, `bzhi`)\\nwithout runtime CPU detection in a package that declares generic `linux-x64`\\nsupport violates this contract.\\n\\n## Comparison evidence (same source, machine, runtime)\\n\\n- Identical input and flags: `oxlint --version` / linting, same reporter\\n  machine, same Node v22.23.3; only the package version differs.\\n- Both package versions pulled from the official npm registry:\\n  `@oxlint/binding-linux-x64-gnu@1.94.0` (works on the reporter's CPU) vs\\n  `@1.95.0` (SIGILLs).\\n- Static comparison of both shipped binaries: the exact reported faulting\\n  sequence (`rorx $0x10,%ecx,%ecx` at file offset `0x27c33c` in\\n  `chacha_block` of mimalloc's `random.c`) is present in 1.95.0 and absent in\\n  1.94.0 (0 vs 150 BMI1/BMI2 instructions binary-wide).\\n- Diagnosis: Ubuntu 26.04 runner image's GCC 15.2 defaults to\\n  `-march=x86-64-v3`; oxc's pinned `cc 1.2.65` lacks the `-march=x86-64`\\n  mitigation;